One Link - for the people
=========================

One Link is a peer-to-peer LAN chat and file-sync tool that runs
without accounts, without servers, and without telemetry. It is
released under the GNU Affero General Public License version 3 or
later (AGPLv3) starting with v0.20.7. See LICENSE for details.

Why AGPLv3 and not MIT
----------------------

The choice of AGPLv3 is deliberate. One Link's threat model is
documented in docs/SECURITY.md: real users on real laptops and
phones, defending against adversaries that include corporate data
collection, network-on-path attackers, lost devices, compromised
peers, compromised mirrors, and (in the hardened tier) state-actor
adversaries. The cryptography and the architecture do that work.
The license does the OTHER half of the work, which is keeping
those defenses in the hands of the people who use One Link.

Permissive licenses (MIT, Apache 2.0, BSD) allow anyone to take
the code, modify it, ship the modified version as a closed-source
product, and never share their changes back. For a privacy tool
that's a structural problem: a corporate fork could quietly remove
a defense, ship the modified binary as "One Link Pro," and
collect data from users who think they're running the upstream
project. The MIT license offers no remedy for this.

AGPLv3 prevents it. Anyone who modifies One Link and offers the
modified version to others - whether by distribution OR by network
service - must offer the corresponding source under AGPLv3 too. A
corporation can still build a product on One Link; they just can't
hide the modifications they make. Users always have the source of
the version they're running, which means they can audit it,
rebuild it, and verify it for themselves.

The "or later" clause means One Link automatically inherits any
future AGPL revision the Free Software Foundation publishes, so
the protection adapts as software-licensing law evolves.

The "for the people" charter
----------------------------

One Link commits to:

  - No accounts. Identity is a local Ed25519 keypair, period.
  - No phone number. No email. No SMS verification.
  - No telemetry, no analytics, no third-party SDK, no
    phone-home of any kind. (See docs/SECURITY.md "We promise"
    section; the codebase is auditable and the test suite pins
    this.)
  - No advertising. Ever.
  - No selling user data. There is no user data on any server
    we control to sell - the architecture forbids it.
  - No corporate substrate dependency that the user can't opt
    out of. Hardware secure-element APIs (Secure Enclave,
    StrongBox, TPM) are local primitives and are used; cloud
    sync services that come with them (iCloud Keychain,
    Google Password Manager) are NOT used by default and never
    transmit identity material to the corporate operator.
  - Reproducible builds, so anyone can verify a release matches
    the source. (Implementation in flight; see
    .github/workflows/reproducible-build.yml.)
  - Multi-maintainer threshold release signing once the project
    grows beyond a single maintainer.
  - Public threat model maintained continuously, with audit
    cadence (annual external pentest target). See
    docs/SECURITY.md.
  - Public bug bounty once funding allows.
  - Warrant canary once relevant.

What you can do with this code (under AGPLv3)
---------------------------------------------

  - Run it for any purpose, commercial or non-commercial.
  - Study, modify, and learn from it.
  - Distribute copies, modified or unmodified.
  - Run it as a network service for other people.

In all of those cases, AGPLv3 requires that recipients of your
distribution OR users of your network service have access to the
corresponding source code under the same license. That's the only
restriction.

What you can NOT do
-------------------

  - Ship a modified version as a closed-source product.
  - Run a modified version as a SaaS / hosted service without
    making the modified source available to your users.
  - Strip or replace this NOTICE or the LICENSE file in
    distributed copies.

History
-------

Versions of One Link prior to commit `87d1b98` (released as
v0.20.7+ in 2026-05) were under the MIT License and remain under
MIT in perpetuity for anyone who has them. The license change at
v0.20.7 reflects the project's growth from "personal experiment"
to "tool for the people."  All NEW contributions and NEW
derivative works from that commit forward are AGPLv3.

If you forked the MIT version, your fork stays under MIT until
you choose to sync with a post-v0.20.7 commit. You may continue
to develop your MIT fork independently in perpetuity.

Trademark + project identity
----------------------------

"One Link" is the project's identity and the working name of the
code in this repository. Trademark policy will be formalized once
the project incorporates as a non-profit foundation. Until then:
forks may use the name "One Link" if they accurately describe
their relationship to upstream (e.g. "One Link fork by Acme,
based on commit abc123") and do not present themselves as the
canonical project.

